# Shell safety

Apply to every shell script. Bash is forgiving in dangerous ways — these defaults catch common bugs.

## Boilerplate every script needs

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail
IFS=$'\n\t'
```

## Always

- Quote variable expansions: `"$var"`, `"${var}"`, `"$@"`
- `[[ ... ]]` over `[ ... ]`
- `(( ... ))` for numeric comparison
- Arrays for argument lists: `args=(...)`, expand `"${args[@]}"`
- `done < <(cmd)` not `cmd | while read`
- `rm -rf "${var:?}/..."` for destructive ops with required var

## Never

- Missing shebang
- Missing `set -euo pipefail`
- Unquoted variable expansion
- `cd dir` without `&& ...` or `|| exit`
- `rm -rf "$var/"` without `${var:?}` guard
- `eval` (almost always wrong)
- Long pipelines without `pipefail`
- `for line in $(cat file)` — use `while IFS= read -r line; do ... done < file`
- `echo` on untrusted data — use `printf '%s\n'`
